		<div class="commentthread_comment_content">
			<div data-panel="{&quot;flow-children&quot;:&quot;row&quot;}" class="commentthread_comment_author">
								<div class="commentthread_comment_avatar playerAvatar offline">
															<a href="https://steamcommunity.com/profiles/76561198041235838" data-miniprofile="80970110">
													<img src="https://avatars.akamai.steamstatic.com/fef49e7fa7e1997310d705b2a6158ff8dc1cdfeb.jpg" srcset="https://avatars.akamai.steamstatic.com/fef49e7fa7e1997310d705b2a6158ff8dc1cdfeb.jpg 1x, https://avatars.akamai.steamstatic.com/fef49e7fa7e1997310d705b2a6158ff8dc1cdfeb_medium.jpg 2x">											</a>
				</div>
				<div class="author_name_group">
					<div class="flex_row">
						<a class="hoverunderline commentthread_author_link " href="https://steamcommunity.com/profiles/76561198041235838" onclick="return Forum_AuthorMenu( this, 80970110, '572679173080117140' );" data-miniprofile="80970110">
							<bdi>Nathan Pascal<span class="forum_author_action_pulldown"></span></bdi></a>

															<div id="author_menu_80970110_572679173080117140" class="popup_block_new forum_author_menu" style="display: none;">
			<div class="popup_body popup_menu">
				<a class="popup_menu_item tight" href="https://steamcommunity.com/profiles/76561198041235838">View Profile</a>
				<a class="popup_menu_item tight" href="https://steamcommunity.com/profiles/76561198041235838/posthistory/">View Posts</a>
															</div>
		</div>
								
						<img src="https://community.akamai.steamstatic.com/public/images/skin_1/icon_InLibrary.png" style="" class="inlibrary" title="has Anywhere RPG">																		
						<div data-panel="{&quot;flow-children&quot;:&quot;row&quot;}" class="community_award_ctn" id="community_awards_comment_572679173080117140"></div>
					</div>
					<div class="commentthread_comment_timestamp">
											</div>
					<div class="commentthread_comment_timestamp" title="October 30, 2026 @ 3:37:54 pm PST" data-timestamp="1793399874">
						Oct 30 @ 3:37pm&nbsp;
					</div>
				</div>

				
				<div class="commentthread_comment_actions" >
														<a class="forum_comment_action forum_comment_action_trigger" data-tooltip-type="selector" data-tooltip-content="#forum_comment_actions_menu_572679173080117140">
					<span><i class="ico16 arrow_down"></i></span>
				</a>
			
			<div class="forum_comment_action_menu" style="display: none;" id="forum_comment_actions_menu_572679173080117140">				<a class="forum_comment_action report tooltip" href="javascript:Forum_ShowReportDialog( '103582791475798685', '590692129307765155', '572679173080109213', '80970110', '572679173080117140' );" data-tooltip-text="Report this post for inappropriate content.  Reported post are flagged for review by moderators.">
				<span class="non_breaking_space">&nbsp;</span><img src="https://community.akamai.steamstatic.com/public/images/skin_1/notification_icon_flag_dark.png?v=1">Report this post</a>
			</div>
							</div>
			</div>
			<div class="commentthread_comment_text" id="comment_content_572679173080117140">
				Your numbers are slightly off, Anywhere RPG is the fourth title by the dev. That said, it maybe makes more sense to consider it one large project to explore the capabilities of AI for emergent gameplay. As the capabilities grow, there is more potential to explore another approach, and some of those work better, some worse.<br><br>I agree that there is a problem with low-effort AI products flooding the market and taking opportunity away from artists and other creators, but I think you are judging this one somewhat incorrectly, as creating frameworks that utilize AI as core functionality is pretty distinct from anything else we have seen in game development in the past, and much different from simply vibe-coding or asset generating a fixed experience.<br><br>If you wish for more perspective, across all of his titles the dev offers a free service to run his AI models to power the game, at his own cost, mostly without limits, which includes the risk that some buyers might eventually cost him more money than he made by selling the game to them. I think the mark of &quot;slop&quot; is trying to cut corners and make quick income, while this one is closer to an indie passion project that chases an elusive vision and wants to share it with some people on the way.			</div>
			<div class="comment_footer_ctn">
									<div></div>
				
				
									<div class="forum_comment_permlink">
